  "content": {
    "title": "Afroman Wins Civil Trial Over Use of Police Raid Footage in His Music Videos",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTSeven law enforcement officers had accused the rapper, whose name is Joseph E. Foreman, of causing them “humiliation” by using images from their 2022 raid on his Ohio home in two music videos.Listen · 3:11 min The rapper Afroman, whose real name is Joseph E. Foreman, performing in Hollywood, Fla., in 2018.Credit...Johnny Louis/Getty ImagesMarch 19, 2026An Ohio jury on Wednesday ruled in favor of the rapper Afroman after a civil trial in which law enforcement officers accused him of causing them mental distress by using footage from their 2022 raid on his home in a pair of music videos.The rapper, whose real name is Joseph E. Foreman, was sued in 2023 by seven officers with the Adams County Sheriff’s Office in southern Ohio. They said in a complaint that Mr. Foreman’s use of their images in the music videos and to promote his brand had caused them to suffer “humiliation, ridicule, mental distress, embarrassment and loss of reputation.”The jury delivered its verdict after a three-day trial that raised questions of policing, free-speech protections and artistic freedom.Mr. Foreman, who is best known for the 2000 song “Because I Got High,” celebrated the verdict in a video posted to social media on Wednesday afternoon.“We did it America! Yeah! We did it — freedom of speech!” Mr. Foreman, 51, said in the video. “Power to the people!” he added.The Adams County Sheriff’s Office did not immediately respond to a request for comment on Wednesday evening.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Livia Albeck-Ripka is a Times reporter based in Los Angeles, covering breaking news, California and other subjects.AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
